    "seo": {
      "title": "Labor Productivity in Iran: Measures and Interpretation",
      "description": "Understand output per worker, output per hour, purchasing power comparisons, and the evidence needed to assess Iran's labor productivity."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "what-productivity-measures",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What productivity measures",
        "content": "Labor productivity relates the value of production to the labor used in producing it. Two common measures divide gross domestic product, or GDP, by the number of employed people or by total hours worked. GDP measures production across the economy; it is not a measure of the wages workers receive. The International Labour Organization, a United Nations agency specializing in work and employment, identifies both workers and hours as possible denominators. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters when reading evidence about Iran. Output per worker can change because production changes, because employment changes, or because both move together. Output per hour additionally accounts for changes in working time. A comparison between the two therefore needs consistent definitions of employment and hours, rather than an assumption that every employed person works an identical schedule. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An indicator of the production system",
        "content": "A productivity statistic should not be read as a score for individual effort. Machinery, technology, intermediate inputs, organization, and economies of scale all affect the output associated with an hour of work. The OECD, an international economic research organization, explicitly cautions that labor productivity only partly reflects workers' personal capacities or effort. [s2]\n\nThis also explains why a rise in aggregate productivity needs interpretation. It can reflect investment and innovation, but it can also accompany a reduction in the employment of workers in activities with lower measured output per worker. A higher average does not, by itself, demonstrate that employment opportunities improved. Productivity and the extent to which an economy uses available labor are related but distinct dimensions of economic performance. [s2]\n\nMultifactor productivity asks a different question: how much output is produced relative to a combination of inputs, commonly labor and capital. Labor productivity can rise when workers have more equipment available, even without an improvement in the efficiency of the combined inputs. These measures should therefore retain their separate names and definitions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-data",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the available Iran series",
        "content": "The World Bank publishes an Iran indicator titled GDP per person employed, measured in constant 2021 purchasing-power-parity dollars. Purchasing power parity, usually abbreviated PPP, provides a basis for comparing purchasing power across economies. The indicator's constant-price basis distinguishes it from a current-dollar measure whose movements can reflect changing prices and currency conversion. Its metadata identifies the figures as staff estimates using employment, population, GDP, and PPP inputs from several international organizations. [s4]\n\nThe ILO also lists output per worker and output per hour in constant 2021 international dollars, alongside a separate constant 2015 US-dollar series. These are distinct statistical products; their price bases and units must remain visible. The ILO explains that its modelled database combines nationally reported information with estimates where information is missing. [s5]\n\nThe inspected pages established the available measures but did not expose a verified annual Iran table suitable for reproduction here. This page consequently makes no claim about the direction or size of Iran's recent productivity trend. A completed assessment needs the observations, release vintage, employment coverage, and any revisions before it can connect movements in the indicator to developments in the Iranian economy. [s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
